# WebGPU Three.js Performance Lessons

Measured on a production scene (three r185, Dawn D3D12). The short version:

- Startup pipeline compilation freezes the main thread; use `passNode.compileAsync(renderer)` after setting `scene.environment`, with frustum culling disabled during the compile.
- Every non-batched light adds code to every shader program. Batch shadowless lights with `DynamicLighting`; avoid `RectAreaLight`; cast shadows from SpotLights only (a PointLight shadow is a 6-face cube, ~6× the cost).
- Colors and scalar values are uniforms and share programs; map presence, `side`, Physical-material features, skinning, instancing, and `receiveShadow` fork new ones. Use `material.clone()` for tint variants.
- `renderer.dispose()` does not destroy the `GPUDevice`; destroy it explicitly and hook `pagehide`, or hot reloads leak devices and every startup gets slower.
- The WebGL2 fallback (no secure context, no WebGPU) compiles synchronously and needs a lighter path: fewer shadows, less overdraw, capped pixelRatio.

